## Is tinnitus a disability?

Under the **Equality Act 2010**, a condition counts as a disability if it has a **substantial** (more than minor) and **long-term** (12 months or more) effect on your normal daily activities. The law looks at how you would be **without treatment**. Severe tinnitus, especially with hearing loss, can be covered.

## At work

Reasonable adjustments could include:

- a quieter workspace, or noise-cancelling headphones
- background sound where you work
- written instructions, if tinnitus makes it hard to hear
- breaks from noisy tasks

## Noise at work

Your employer must protect you from loud noise, for example by reducing noise and giving you **ear protection**. If you were an employee and developed hearing loss from certain noisy jobs, you may be able to claim **Industrial Injuries Disablement Benefit**.

## Benefits

Tinnitus on its own rarely qualifies for PIP. If you also have hearing loss, read about [hearing loss, work and benefits](/conditions/brain-nerves-and-senses/hearing-loss/benefits-and-work/).

Free, independent advisers, such as **Citizens Advice** or a local welfare rights service, can help you check what you can claim and fill in forms.
